🔹 1. Use DTO Projection (Best practice for skipping columns in response)

Instead of returning the full entity, you map only required columns into a DTO.

Entity

@Entity
@Table(name = "person")
public class Person {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private String name;

    private int age;

    private String address; // you want to skip this in response

    // getters & setters
}


DTO (only selected fields)

public class PersonDTO {
    private Long id;
    private String name;

    public PersonDTO(Long id, String name) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }

    // getters
}


Repository with JPQL Projection

public interface PersonRepository extends JpaRepository<Person, Long> {

    @Query("SELECT new com.example.demo.dto.PersonDTO(p.id, p.name) FROM Person p")
    List<PersonDTO> findAllPersonDTOs();
}


👉 Now findAllPersonDTOs() will only fetch id and name, skipping age and address.

🔹 2. Use Spring Data Projection (Interface-based)

If you don’t want a DTO class, you can use an interface projection:

public interface PersonView {
    Long getId();
    String getName();
}

public interface PersonRepository extends JpaRepository<Person, Long> {
    List<PersonView> findAllBy();
}


👉 JPA will fetch only id and name columns.

🔹 3. Use @JsonIgnore (only for hiding in JSON response, not DB fetch)

If you want the column available in DB but not visible in API response (e.g., hiding password):

@Entity
public class Person {
    @Id
    private Long id;

    private String name;

    @JsonIgnore   // will not appear in response JSON
    private String address;
}


👉 This still fetches address from DB, but it won’t be sent in JSON response.

🔹 4. Use @Transient if column should not exist in DB

If the field should never be persisted or fetched:

@Transient
private String tempData;


✅ Recommendation:

If you want to skip fetching from DB → Use DTO or Projection.

If you just want to hide in response → Use @JsonIgnore.